From: Eduardo Garcia-Madico Portabella <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 10:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: qrtr: ns: say which server the del-client failed on, once

On a device where one local server never accepts the del-client command,
these two messages repeat forever -- measured at one pair every 10
seconds, indefinitely, on an SM7150 phone.

Rate limiting does not help: the default budget passes a message every 10
seconds without ever tripping. The result is a console ring buffer made
entirely of this:

    failed to send del client cmd
    failed while handling packet from 1:16457

That matters beyond the noise. The ramoops console buffer is what
survives a crash, and on this device it is 1 MB. After an overnight hang
it held 11600 lines of which 234 were anything else, so a hung task
report from hours earlier had already been pushed out by the time the
machine was recovered.

Report each of them once, and say which server the send failed on and
with what error. As it stood the message named neither the destination
nor the errno, so a permanently stuck endpoint was indistinguishable
from a transient one and there was no way to tell which of the local
servers was refusing -- the very things needed to fix it.

The condition is permanent when it happens, so the first occurrence
carries all the information the repeats do, and the error is still
returned to the caller either way.
--- a/net/qrtr/ns.c	2026-08-14 09:55:15.046872928 +0200
+++ b/net/qrtr/ns.c	2026-08-14 10:02:47.419052640 +0200
@@ -489,7 +489,8 @@
 
 		ret = kernel_sendmsg(qrtr_ns.sock, &msg, &iv, 1, sizeof(pkt));
 		if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV) {
-			pr_err("failed to send del client cmd\n");
+			pr_err_once("failed to send del client cmd to %d:%d: %d\n",
+				     srv->node, srv->port, ret);
 			return ret;
 		}
 	}
@@ -719,7 +720,7 @@
 		}
 
 		if (ret < 0)
-			pr_err_ratelimited("failed while handling packet from %d:%d",
+			pr_err_once("failed while handling packet from %d:%d",
 			       sq.sq_node, sq.sq_port);
 	}
 
